# Container Environment

You are running inside an isolated Docker container. Whether your workspace under `/workspace` reaches the host depends on the launch mode: in **dir-mounted** mode (the default) it is a host bind mount, so edits land on the user's disk; in **self-hosted** mode (`--isolated`) it is a container-local Docker volume holding a clone, so nothing written there is visible to the user and work normally leaves only by `git push` / a PR.
Check the mode at runtime with the `POWBOX_WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH` env var — set and non-empty means a host bind mount (its value is the workspace's host path), unset means container-local. Use it before you promise the user a local file — and note that `.worktrees/` is on container-local storage, so a file inside a task worktree is not on the user's disk either. `/ctx/<name>` folders are host bind mounts too (read-only unless launched `:rw`).

You have full autonomy (`${AGENT_AUTONOMY_FLAG}`) — the container and its network firewall are your safety boundary. When Claude is the primary agent, Claude Code's leftover `rm` permission prompts are off too (`CLAUDE_CODE_EVEN_MORE_DANGEROUSLY_SKIP_PERMISSIONS=1` is set at launch), so you may clean up your own probe and temp directories without waiting on a prompt. That covers the launched session and its in-process subagents only: a `claude` started from a session's shell (`claude -p`, a peer review) is a child session that keeps those prompts, so an `rm` in a prompt you hand one needs literal paths or guarded expansions such as `"${DIR:?}"`. The same variable would lift Claude in Chrome's safeguards in the user's real browser, so on such a launch Claude in Chrome is off by default too (`CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_CFC=0`); do not start Claude with `--chrome` unless the user asks you to.

Your identity for this session is in `$CONTAINER_NAME` (also available via the `cid` command).

## Reference files

Detail that most sessions never need is kept out of this file. It is baked into the image at `/usr/local/share/agent-reference/`, where it describes the image you are running in. Read a file when its situation arises:

- `tooling.md` — before concluding that a tool is missing or installing one, before first using a linter, a document converter (PDF, Word, slides) or a tool a layer set adds, and before debugging a Claude Workflow script or run (`wf-check`, `wf-status`): what is baked, pinned versions, caveats.
- `git-helpers.md` — before a cross-branch read, before fetching PR review threads, and before using a disposable clone: `gitcat`, `gh-review-threads` and `dc-enter`/`dc-remove` in full.
- `worktree-helpers.md` — when `wt-bootstrap` blocks or `wt-enter`/`wt-remove` refuses, and before removing or cleaning up a worktree any other way: what each helper checks and why.
- `environment.md` — when it matters whether a path is a volume, a tmpfs shadow or a host mount, what survives container recreation or where a cache lives, and before asking for a firewall exception.
- `known-limitations.md` — before reporting friction from a run, in a retrospective or a session-learnings report: leave out what it lists, or report only what is new about it.

This file is rendered onto a config volume that containers built from other images share, so it can name a file your image does not have. A missing file means the image predates it or lacks the layer set that contributes it: fall back to the tool's `--help` and to `command -v`.

## Tooling

`node`, `npm`, `pnpm`, `python3`, `pip3`, `git`, `gh`, `jq`, `rg`, `fd`, `make` and `gcc` are on `PATH`, with the usual shell, lint, archive and document tools (`tooling.md` lists them). A layer set can add more: when the image that last rendered this file was built with one, its notes are the last section here, "Additional tooling from the … layer set". Check with `command -v` before concluding that a tool is missing, and before relying on one those notes name.

- The baked `yq` is the jq wrapper: its filters are jq syntax, not the Go tool's.
- `markdownlint-cli2` exits zero when its globs match no files, so a wrong path or an over-broad ignore reads as a clean pass. Check the file count it prints.
- Install more with `sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y <package>` (a wrapper for the configured repositories only; `sudo apt-get --help` lists what it accepts) or `pip3 install <package>` (no `--break-system-packages` needed).

## Shell hazards

- A pipeline reports the status of its last command, so a failure before the pipe is hidden: `git clone … | tail` succeeds when the clone failed. Check the command itself, or `set -o pipefail`, before anything depends on it.
- A state-changing command chained after an unchecked `cd` (`cd "$dir"; git commit …`) runs wherever the shell was left when the `cd` fails, and agents have run git commands against the shared main checkout this way. Write `cd "$dir" && …`, or `git -C <absolute path> …`.

## Delegating to another agent

This container ships more than one agent harness. Besides yourself, the following peer agent(s) are installed, on `PATH`, and already configured (each keeps its own persistent login and skills in this container):

${AGENT_PEERS}

Invoke a peer directly when it helps — e.g. hand a self-contained sub-task to the other agent (for a review, use `peer-review-run` below). A peer shares this container and its `/workspace` tree (bind mount or self-hosted volume alike), so it sees the same files you do. Run it non-interactively for a one-shot task (the one-shot forms above; a single invocation runs a full agentic loop, so one prompt is enough), or interactively to hand off the session. Keep the `< /dev/null` those forms end in: a peer started with stdin left open waits on it before it starts, and `codex exec` waits for good.

**For a peer review, run `peer-review-run`** (on `PATH`; usage in `peer-review-run --help`) instead of a one-shot form: it feeds the prompt from a file, supervises the provider under a deadline, reaps its process tree, and returns one normalized result. Size a blocking wait by the pairings its `--help` gives, because a call your harness kills at its foreground cap returns no result and may leave the provider running. Stop any peer by waiting on the helper or on a PID you recorded, never by a process-name pattern. Do not count on a reviewer reaching the network (a read-only Codex sandbox has none, and the helper gives Claude no shell or web tool), so put PR threads, CI logs and anything else it needs from the network into its prompt.

Peer opinions are **best-effort**: if the binary is missing, the login preflight fails, or it reports exhausted usage, treat that as "no second opinion available" and continue on your own judgment — don't block or error out. Retry a raw launch only on a clearly transient failure, once or twice; `peer-review-run` already retries within its `--attempts`. When delegating a review, give the peer the concrete scope (base branch or commit range, worktree path) and ask for numbered findings and a closing bare `VERDICT: PASS` or `VERDICT: ISSUES` line, the only form `peer-review-run` reads a verdict from.

### Scratchpad discipline

The session scratchpad is **one directory shared by every concurrently running subagent** — "session-specific" means the session, not the subagent.
So every file parallel work writes there MUST carry a per-task unique name (suffix the worktree slug or the PR number) or live in a `mktemp -d -p <scratchpad>` subdirectory — a `$$` PID suffix does not survive to the next tool call, which gets a fresh shell.
Fixed names have already crossed streams here: two reviewers both writing `verify.log`, one then validating the other's worktree.
When you fan work out, state this rule in each subagent's prompt — that is the only way it learns it has siblings.

## Git and GitHub

- GitHub authentication is **HTTPS** through the `gh` credential helper; there are no SSH keys. An SSH-form `origin` (`git@github.com:…`) works as it is for `git push`, `git fetch` and `gh`, through a container-local `insteadOf` rewrite, so do not rewrite the remote: in a host-mounted workspace that edits the user's repository.
- The global git config is re-copied from the host at every container start, so hand edits to it do not survive a restart.
- Never rewrite published history with raw `git push --force`/`-f`: a concurrent push to the old tip would be silently clobbered. Use `git push --force-with-lease`, ideally the exact `--force-with-lease=<branch>:<sha>`, and keep one writer per PR branch.
- Fetch a PR's review threads with `gh-review-threads <PR#>`, not a hand-rolled `reviewThreads` query: run beside other `gh` GraphQL calls, `gh api graphql --paginate` has returned **another PR's** threads.
- `gitcat <ref> <path> [<start> [<end>]]` prints a file as of another branch or commit, with stable line numbers and without switching branches.
- A check that wants to delete refs, rewrite history, `gc` or run a destructive command goes in a **disposable clone**: `DC="$(dc-enter <slug>)"`, dropped with `dc-remove <slug>`. Never run it in the checkout itself or in an improvised clone-and-`cd`. A worktree is **not** a substitute: it shares `.git`, so `branch -f`, `reset`, `update-ref`, `gc` and `push` reach every sibling worktree. Three things about the pair fail silently:
  - `dc-enter` prints only the clone's path on stdout, and nothing there when it fails, so check `$DC` before using it (`[ -n "$DC" ] && [ -d "$DC/.git" ] || exit 1`). An unguarded empty `$DC` in `rm -rf "$DC"/*` is the incident the pair exists to prevent. A slug that already has a clone is refused, not reused.
  - `GIT_DIR` and its relatives override `git -C`, so while they are set `git -C "$DC" …` hits the invoking repository. `unset` them first (`git rev-parse --local-env-vars` prints the set).
  - `dc-remove` finds a clone only from the worktree that ran `dc-enter`. From anywhere else it says there is nothing to remove and succeeds, leaving the clone and its slug in place.

### Worktrees

Parallel, worktree-isolated work uses three baked helpers. Prefer them to raw `git worktree` commands: they keep worktrees on container-local storage under `.worktrees/$CONTAINER_NAME/` and never delete work.

- `wt-bootstrap` — run it once per session before creating worktrees. It prints one JSON object, and a non-zero exit means STOP.
- `wt-enter <slug> <branch> [<base>]` — reuse, attach or create the task worktree and print only its path (`WT="$(wt-enter …)"`). Omit `<base>` for a stage that must never create work, such as a reviewer attaching to an implementer's worktree: a missing branch is then an error, not a silent empty checkout.
- `wt-remove <slug> [--force]` — remove a task worktree, but never work: even with `--force` it refuses one with uncommitted changes, a git operation in progress, or a state it cannot establish. A refusal names what it found. Inspect that, and do not answer it with `git worktree remove --force`, which discards all of it. The branch is always kept.

Commit and push rather than trusting a worktree to survive a container recycle.

**Shared vs. isolated surfaces (read before any cleanup or discard).** The helpers scope worktree creation, pruning and removal by `$CONTAINER_NAME`, but the `.worktrees` storage **and the repository's main checkout are shared by every process in this container**, a peer harness you invoke included. So when working in parallel, keep every edit and cleanup inside your assigned worktree (`git -C <your-worktree> …`) and commit checkpoints often. Ordinary Git recovery in a worktree that is yours alone is fine. Run against the shared main checkout, or any tree another agent is working in, a broad `git reset --hard` or `git clean -fdx` (which ignores `.gitignore` and so also wipes the `.worktrees` scaffolding) discards a concurrent peer's uncommitted files, and so does a path-level discard: `git checkout -- <path>`, `git restore <path>` and `git stash` have destroyed a sibling's uncommitted work as surely as a hard reset would. If you genuinely need one there, pause or finish sibling work first.

A worktree's `node_modules` is not private scratch either. Installs hardlink from the shared store, so editing a file under `node_modules` in place edits every worktree's copy. A symlink to another checkout's `node_modules` is not matched by a `node_modules/` ignore pattern, which matches directories only, so it ends up staged. In both cases the answer is to run the install in the worktree.

## Workspace storage

- In dir-mounted mode a JS project's `node_modules` (the root's and each sub-package's) and each .NET project's `bin`/`obj` are container-local shadows over the host bind mount, so container build output stays off the user's disk. They are detected at container start and again before every `pnpm` install. After adding a .NET project mid-session, or before populating a new sub-package's `node_modules` by any means other than `pnpm`, run `shadow-refresh.sh` first, or the output lands on the host.
- When an install reports nothing to do while a package's binaries (`vitest`, `tsc`, `eslint`, …) are missing, run `pnpm-shadow-doctor` (`--fix` repairs it). No flavor of reinstall does.

## Network

The container firewall blocks private and local networks (10.x, 172.16.x, 192.168.x, link-local, IPv6 unique-local) and CGNAT (100.64.0.0/10, which includes Tailscale addresses), and a blocked connection times out rather than being refused. The public internet is open.

`$POWBOX_EGRESS_ALLOW` lists any exceptions the user opened, as `proto,destination,port` entries separated by `;`, and is unset when there are none. They are fixed when the container is created and are the user's policy: when you need another destination, ask the user to add it to `.powbox-egress` rather than working around the firewall.
